# Reactivity at the backend/OS boundary in `~/dev/invar`

## 0. The headline shape

There is **exactly one filesystem watcher in the entire application**, and it belongs to git.

```
$ grep -rn "from 'node:fs'" src/ --include=*.ts | grep -v test   # 29 files
$ ... | grep -w watch                                            # 1 file
src/modules/git/GitWatcher.ts:17: import { watch, readdirSync, lstatSync, type FSWatcher } from 'node:fs';
```

No `chokidar`, no `fs.watchFile`, no Bun `--watch` equivalent, no recursive watch. The file tree, the editor buffers, the settings file, the tasks tree and the database file are **not** watched. Freshness outside git is bought with three other currencies:

| currency | where |
|---|---|
| **mtime as a cache key** | `Files.mtimeMs` → `GitBlameCache`, `TextDocument`, `TasksDashboardOverview` stamps |
| **observation-gated interval** (`$watch(() => isObserved())` → `setInterval`) | `MonitoringStats`, `TasksDashboardOverview` |
| **manual / event-driven invalidate** | `FileTree.refresh()` (a command), `Settings.load()`, LSP `syncDocument` from a targeted `$watch` |

And the *bridge* from imperative OS-land into the reactive graph is one idiom, used everywhere:

> **A non-reactive owner mutates plain ground truth (a `Map`, a `Set`, an array), then bumps a single `ref(0)` counter. Reactive getters begin with `void this.<x>Revision.value;` to subscribe without reading it.**

19 subscribe sites, ~50 revision/version signal declarations. Counted below in §9.

---

## 1. `Files` — the FS capability is deliberately *outside* the graph

`~/dev/invar/src/modules/system/Files.ts` — 266 lines, `Static($Files)`, **zero refs, zero state**. Every method is a synchronous `node:fs` call. It is the single home for the path-traversal boundary (`confineToRoot`, L9).

The load-bearing comment is on `mtimeMs` (`Files.ts:42-51`):

```ts
/** Last-modification time in epoch milliseconds, or 0 when the path cannot be stat'd. Used as a cheap
 *  freshness key: a save bumps mtime, so a cache keyed on it invalidates exactly when the file changes
 *  on disk (and a cursor move — no disk change — is a pure cache hit). */
static mtimeMs(path: string): number {
  try { return statSync(path).mtimeMs; } catch { return 0; }
}
```

Note `listNames` vs `list` (`Files.ts:101-139`): `listNames` is one `readdir` with **no per-entry stat** — "the cheap, non-blocking primitive the open-project navigator caps and classifies itself, so it never stats an unbounded number of entries up front." `list()` does stat every entry (`isDir`) and is what `FileTree` uses.

Also here: `replaceBytesIfUnchanged` (`Files.ts:149-187`) — an optimistic-concurrency file write (read → compare bytes → temp file with `flag: 'wx'` → `rename` → read-back verify), returning `{replaced, reason}` where reason ∈ `'changed' | 'read-only' | 'read-back' | <errno message>`. This is find/replace's guard against writing over a file that changed under it. Never throws.

Contract: `src/modules/system/system.invariants.md` → *Capability classes are stateless and Static wrapped* (:49), *File access is confined to a single root* (:120).

---

## 2. `GitWatcher` — the only watcher, and the notify/pull-floor contract

`~/dev/invar/src/modules/git/GitWatcher.ts` (449 lines). **Not reactive at all** — a plain `Static($GitWatcher)` instance class with `FSWatcher` handles and timers. It never touches a ref. It talks to reactivity only through `repository.refresh()` (which writes refs) and two callbacks.

### Watch-set establishment: breadth-first, ignore-pruned, paint-deferred

```ts
// GitWatcher.ts:137-158
protected async establishWatchSet(viewPainted?: Promise<void>): Promise<void> {
  // Leave construction immediately so workspace activation can paint before any repository walk or
  // Git subprocess begins.
  await (viewPainted ?? this.yieldToEventLoop());
  if (this.disposed) return;
  this.startReconcileFloor();
  await Promise.all([
    this.establishWorkingTreeWatchSet(this.cwd, true),
    this.establishHeadWatch(),
  ]);
  ...
  this.watchSetEstablished = true;
  this.onWatchSetEstablished?.();
}
```

`walkAndWatchByLevel` (`:176-216`) does one **bulk** `git check-ignore -z --stdin` subprocess *per depth level*, not per directory — so subprocess cost tracks tree **depth**, not width, and `await this.yieldToEventLoop()` (`setImmediate`) between levels keeps the walk off the frame.

Rationale in the file header (`:1-15`): a single recursive root watch would open a handle per directory inside `node_modules`, and "on this platform Bun's recursive watch does not even reliably deliver nested events."

### The HEAD watch is a deliberate second channel

```ts
// GitWatcher.ts:62-97 (comment abridged)
// We watch the git DIR — not the HEAD file — because git replaces HEAD by rename (HEAD.lock -> HEAD),
// which would break a file watch after the first switch; a directory watch survives it.
const result = await this.Processes.run(['git','rev-parse','--absolute-git-dir'], this.cwd);
...
this.headWatcher = watch(gitDirectory, (_event, changedName) => {
  if (changedName === null || changedName === 'HEAD' || changedName.toString() === 'HEAD') {
    this.scheduleRefresh();
  }
});
this.headWatcher.unref?.();
```

A `null` filename (platform couldn't report it) is treated as "maybe HEAD" and refreshes — fail-open.

### Debounce: one resettable timer for a whole event storm

```ts
// GitWatcher.ts:364-377  invariant: The watcher has one disposable debounce
protected scheduleRefresh(): void {
  if (this.disposed) return;
  if (this.debounceTimer) clearTimeout(this.debounceTimer);
  this.debounceTimer = setTimeout(() => this.flushRefresh(), this.debounceMilliseconds); // default 80ms
  this.debounceTimer.unref?.();
}
```

Event *payloads are ignored entirely* — no correctness dependency on event names or filenames for the refresh decision (only for the "is this a new subdirectory to watch?" decision).

### The pull floor: `setInterval(scheduleRefresh, 5000)`

```ts
// GitWatcher.ts:237-247  invariant: The git panel converges without watcher notifications
protected startReconcileFloor(): void {
  if (this.disposed || this.reconcileTimer) return;
  this.reconcileTimer = setInterval(() => this.scheduleRefresh(), this.reconcileIntervalMilliseconds);
  this.reconcileTimer.unref?.();
}
```

This is the concrete realization of the project-level invariant **"A notify channel cannot report its own silence"** (`project.invariants.md:204-236`), which is the most interesting contract in the repo:

> "an fs watcher ... can never report a DROPPED event or a DEAD source: silence is indistinguishable from 'nothing changed' and from 'the source hung.' So correctness under any notify channel requires a periodic PULL floor that re-reads ground truth on a bounded interval; notify is a latency accelerator over that floor, never a replacement for it."
> …
> **Evidence:** GitWatcher reconcile-floor …; `scripts/fleet-heartbeat.sh` — *a worker once hung 1.5h SILENTLY under notify-only until a pull heartbeat caught it.* The domain-crossing (fs-watch ⇄ orchestration) is the validator that this is real, not a local convention.

### Error paths all fail *toward* a refresh

- `watchDirectory` catch → `scheduleRefresh()` (`:231-234`)
- `readdirSync` failure mid-walk → `scheduleRefresh()` (`:199-201`)
- catastrophic walk failure → `scheduleRefresh()` + one non-recursive root watch (`:164-171`)
- `watcher.on('error')` → close+delete that handle, `scheduleRefresh()` (`:393-398`)

### The callback may never throw

```ts
// GitWatcher.ts:306-312
protected async onDirectoryEvent(directory, changedName): Promise<void> {
  // Nothing in this body may THROW: fs.watch invokes it outside any caller's try/catch, so an
  // escaped exception (e.g. a filesystem race) would take down the whole process.
  try { ... }
```

And runtime-created entries use `lstatSync(..., { throwIfNoEntry: false })`, **never `stat`** (`:334-346`): "stat() follows links, so a symlink to .git or to an external tree would be recursively watched, and a self-referential link makes stat() throw ELOOP."

### Chained follow-up rides the existing cadence

```ts
// GitWatcher.ts:379-391
protected flushRefresh(): void {
  this.debounceTimer = null;
  if (this.disposed) return;
  void this.repository.refresh({ background: true }).then(() => {
    // The follow-up (log tip-SHA probe) runs only after the status reconcile LANDED, so it reads
    // fresh ground truth (head) — and never after disposal (no zombie refresh chain).
    if (!this.disposed) this.onReconciled?.();
  });
}
```

The `onReconciled` doc comment (`:436-439`) states the design rule explicitly: *"The owner hangs cheap follow-up checks here … so they ride the existing reconcile cadence instead of owning a second timer."*

Contracts: `src/modules/git/git.invariants.md` → *Filesystem notifications arrive in bursts* (:34), *The watcher has one disposable debounce* (:140), *The git panel converges without watcher notifications* (:167), *The watcher never watches inside an ignored directory* (:203).

---

## 3. Watcher → ref bridge in `GitWorkspace`

`~/dev/invar/src/modules/git/GitWorkspace.ts` (982 lines, `Reactive($Class)`).

```ts
// GitWorkspace.ts:262-284
protected activateResources(): void {
  const repository = this.repository.value;
  if (!repository) return;
  this.watcher?.dispose();
  const viewPainted = this.workspace.nextViewPaint();
  let watcher: GitWatcher.Model;
  watcher = new GitWatcher.Class(this.workspace.root, repository, {
    onReconciled: () => {
      void this.reconcileLogTip();
      const activeHandle = this.workspace.activeDocumentHandle;
      if (activeHandle) void this.refreshDocumentHead(activeHandle);
    },
    onWatchSetEstablished: () => {
      if (this.watcher === watcher) this.watcherStatusRevision.value += 1;   // ← the bridge
    },
    viewPainted,
  });
  this.watcher = watcher;
  void viewPainted.then(() => { if (this.watcher === watcher) void repository.refresh(); });
}
```

Note the `this.watcher === watcher` identity guard in both callbacks — a superseded watcher's late callback is discarded rather than cancelled.

The read side (`:385-399`):

```ts
get activationIgnoreQuerySubprocessCount(): number {
  void this.watcherStatusRevision.value;
  return this.watcher?.activationIgnoreQuerySubprocesses ?? 0;
}
```

Three getters, one signal. The `onWatchSetEstablished` doc says why: *"The workspace uses it to publish activation counters without making this resource owner reactive."*

**Lifecycle:** `suspended()` (`:230`) disposes watcher + blame cache and invalidates every document state; `resumed()` (`:236`) reconstructs. This implements `workspace.invariants.md:427` **"N open workspaces do not cost N live GitWatchers"** — only the *active* workspace owns watch handles.

**Paint barrier:** `workspace.invariants.md:456` **"Workspace activation is view-only"** — status *established* (not provisional), verified by `smoke-workspace-tabs-harness.ts`. The first frame after a workspace switch awaits neither the walk nor `git status`.

**`renderVersion` (`:934-963`)** is a hand-rolled aggregate subscriber — ~20 `void x.value` reads, then `return Date.now()`. Deliberately returns a always-changing value so the pane's render revision is "anything moved".

---

## 4. Git freshness, staleness, and quiescence

### Request-ID stale-drop (`GitRepository.ts`)

`~/dev/invar/src/modules/git/GitRepository.ts` — refs are `ref('')`/`shallowRef([])` getters; `refreshRequestId`, `historyRequestId`, `operationId` are plain non-reactive counters.

```ts
// GitRepository.ts:74-90
async refresh(options: GitRefreshOptions = {}): Promise<void> {
  const background = options.background === true;
  if (background && (this.backgroundRefreshInFlight || this.refreshing.value)) return;   // coalesce
  if (background) this.backgroundRefreshInFlight = true;
  const requestId = ++this.refreshRequestId;
  if (!background) { this.refreshing.valu

… [26,630 characters removed from the sample] …

structure/StructureOutline.ts:59-76`. Different flavor: instead of watching `isObserved()` alone, observation is one field of a composite string:

```ts
// :168-176
protected get refreshFingerprint(): string {
  return [
    this.isObserved() ? 'observed' : 'hidden',
    document?.path ?? '',
    this.workspace.providers.revision.value,
    document?.revision.value ?? -1,
  ].join(':');
}
```
```ts
// :63-75
this.$watch(() => this.refreshFingerprint, (fingerprint, previousFingerprint) => {
  const revisionOnly = typeof previousFingerprint === 'string' &&
    this.fingerprintWithoutRevision(fingerprint) === this.fingerprintWithoutRevision(previousFingerprint);
  this.scheduleRefresh(revisionOnly ? this.editRefreshDelay : this.switchRefreshDelay);
}, { immediate: true });
```
**The debounce window is chosen by *which part* of the fingerprint changed**: 350 ms if only the document revision moved (typing), 30 ms otherwise (pane shown, file switched, provider installed). `refresh()` re-checks `if (!this.isObserved()) return;` after `++this.requestGeneration` (`:190-192`) — so a queued timer that fires after the pane hides bumps the generation (invalidating anything in flight) and does nothing else.

The `immediate: true` rationale is worth quoting (`:59-62`): *"the outline can be constructed after the boot state has already settled (document open, pane revealed by the default-visibility policy), so the initial fingerprint is already the final one and no change would ever fire."*

---

## 10. Other backend-adjacent facts

**`Processes`** (`src/modules/system/Processes.ts`, `Static`, 100 lines) — the single subprocess seam. `run()` never throws: a missing binary or nonzero exit yields `{ code: -1 | n, ok: false }`. The hermetic-environment stripper is a real bug fix (`:33-46`):

```ts
/** ... an ambient GIT_DIR / GIT_INDEX_FILE / GIT_WORK_TREE (set, for example, when the app or the
 *  test suite is spawned from inside a git hook) would OVERRIDE that cwd and make `git` operate on
 *  the parent repo instead of the one at `cwd` — a hermeticity break. */
for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(process.env)) {
  if (value === undefined || key.startsWith('GIT_')) continue;
  environment[key] = value;
}
```
The PTY is explicitly *outside* this seam (documented at `:11-16`) because the interactive terminal must inherit the user's full environment. Contract: `system.invariants.md:83` *External tools share one launch policy*.

**`TextDocument` external-change detection** (`src/modules/text/TextDocument.ts:235-260`) — mtime is captured into `savedFileModificationTime` at every load/save, and `matchesSaved()` uses it as the *last* resort in a cheapest-first ladder: clean-revision equality → line count → content length → FNV-1a signature → mtime → full re-read and compare. No watcher; the check happens when someone asks whether the buffer is dirty.

**Workspace search** (`src/modules/search/WorkspaceSearchBackend.ts`) — `rg` subprocess, `this.cancel()` first thing in `search()` (`:104`), an `ActiveWorkspaceSearch { process, cancelled }` record, and a streaming reader loop `while (!activeSearch.cancelled)` (`:163+`) that decodes incrementally and emits per-file callbacks. Absent `rg` degrades to `state: 'unavailable'` with an actionable message rather than an error.

**`Settings`** — no file watcher. `schemaRevision` (`:500`) is bumped by contribution registration (`:618`, `:627`) and subscribed at `:633`. Config on disk is read on `load()` and written on `save()`.

**`StatusChannel` / `GraphChannel`** (`src/modules/system/`) — the observability side channel writes `artifacts/status.json` **atomically** (`writeFileSync` to `.tmp` + `renameSync`) and only when `TUI_OBSERVE=1` or `TUI_STATUS_PATH` is set (`StatusChannel.ts:18-26`): *"Per-frame disk writes are harness infrastructure; normal runs retain in-memory state only."* Contract: `system.invariants.md:143` *Observability never crashes the app*, `:166` *Graph observation reads and never mutates*.

**`AgentSession`** — 12 `renderRevision.value++` sites (`:203`…`:527`), same coarse-signal idiom applied to an SDK/subprocess stream.

---

## 11. Is there one idiom? Yes — three, actually, cleanly separated

### Idiom A: "revision bridge" — imperative producer → `ref(0)` bump → tracked getter

**Bump sites** (non-test), by module: `TextDocument` 20, `AgentSession` 12, `MediaPaneContent` 6, `TerminalInstance` 5, `TerminalPaneContent` 3, `ApplicationContributions` 3, `LanguageClient` 4, `GitDocumentState` 2, `Settings` 2, `OverlayLayer`/`HoverCard`/`BoundedListPopup` 3, `GitBlameCache` 1, `GitWorkspace` 1, `GitComparisonContent` 1.

**Subscribe sites** — the literal `void this.<x>Revision.value;` prologue, 19 non-test occurrences:

```
src/modules/git/GitBlameCache.ts:63              src/modules/lsp/LanguageClient.ts:184,250,321,336
src/modules/git/GitComparisonSurface.ts:52       src/modules/app/ApplicationContributions.ts:59
src/modules/git/GitWorkspace.ts:388,393,398      src/modules/settings/Settings.ts:633
src/modules/plugins/ProviderRegistry.ts:31,42    src/modules/keybindings/KeybindingRegistry.ts:348
src/modules/diff/DiffView.ts:1413,1422           src/modules/ui/OverlayLayer.ts:1360
src/modules/markdown/MarkdownSplitView.ts:549    src/modules/agent/AgentPaneContent.ts:115
```

Backend-facing members of that list: GitBlameCache (fs mtime + git subprocess), GitWorkspace ×3 (fs watcher), LanguageClient ×4 (LSP subprocess), ProviderRegistry ×2 (plugin registration), Settings (config).

Backing this: `project.invariants.md:267` *Cost tracks the actively observed set* → component **"Ground truth is compact and non-reactive at rest — columnar typed arrays / plain Maps / packed spans hold the data; refs are sparse version signals, not value holders."**

### Idiom B: "observation owns the resource" — `$watch(() => isObserved())`

3 sites (`DatabaseConsumerWorkspace:28`, `MonitoringStats:116`, `TasksDashboardOverview:151`) + 1 variant (`StructureOutline:64`, observation folded into a fingerprint). All bottom out in `ApplicationContributions.ts:201`'s `isPainted()`. Resources released: a SQLite connection, a sampling `setInterval`, two `setInterval`s, a debounce `setTimeout`.

Backing: `project.invariants.md:42` *A referenced resource stays alive* → *"A resource lives only while observed"*; component of *Cost tracks the actively observed set* → **"Background work follows paint — a pane owns no timer when its exact content is not painted."**

### Idiom C: "generation counter + late-guard" for every async backend result

Named counters: `refreshRequestId`/`historyRequestId`/`operationId` (GitRepository), `refreshGeneration`/`browseGeneration` (DatabaseConsumerWorkspace), `requestGeneration` (GitDocumentState, StructureOutline), `activationGeneration` (LanguageClient), `logTipProbeGeneration` (GitWorkspace), `requestRevision` capture ×5 + `lastSentVersion` (LanguageClient), `ActiveWorkspaceSearch.cancelled` (search).

Backing: `project.invariants.md:103` *An async result can outlive the state it described* (reality) → `:865` *Async results are revision-stamped and stale results discarded* (chosen).

**The three compose:** C guards the write, A publishes it, B decides whether the machinery exists at all.

---

## 12. Surprises worth calling out

1. **The file tree is the one obviously stale surface.** One fs watcher exists; it serves git only. External file creation/deletion never reaches the tree — `FileTree.refresh()` has exactly one caller, the `files.refresh` command. And `filetree.invariants.md` contains no freshness invariant, so this is *unstated* rather than *chosen-and-documented* — everywhere else in this repo the tradeoff is written down.

2. **The polling floor is designed not to wake the renderer.** `GitRepository.fileRecordsMatch` exists solely so a 5-second `git status` writes zero refs when nothing changed. Without it, the notify-floor design would have cost a repaint every 5 s forever. This is the non-obvious prerequisite that makes "notify + pull floor" compatible with a demand-driven render loop.

3. **"A notify channel cannot report its own silence"** is validated across a domain boundary — fs-watch *and* the build's own parallel worker fleet (`scripts/fleet-heartbeat.sh`), with the anecdote that a worker hung 1.5 h silently under notify-only. The invariant doc treats the domain crossing as the evidence that the rule is real rather than a local convention.

4. **`lstat`, never `stat`, in the watcher's runtime path** (`GitWatcher.ts:334-346`) — three distinct failure modes named: symlink→`.git`, symlink→external tree, self-referential symlink causing `stat()` ELOOP. Plus the whole callback is exception-guarded because `fs.watch` invokes it outside any try/catch and a throw kills the process.

5. **Bulk `git check-ignore -z --stdin` per depth level**, not per directory (`GitWatcher.ts:174-216`) — subprocess count follows tree *depth*. The activation counters (`activationIgnoreQuerySubprocesses`, `activationWatchedDirectories`) are surfaced through the revision bridge specifically so a harness can assert this, and the smoke test compares a *tiny* vs a *wide* fixture.

6. **The `$stopEffects()` asymmetry.** `DatabaseConsumerWorkspace.disposed()` calls it; `LanguageClient.dispose()` deliberately does **not**, with the reason spelled out at `LanguageClient.ts:532` and in `lsp.invariants.md:330`: it owns no watch effects, and calling it would clear the cached getter cells and discard the terminal `disposed` status. An ivue-mechanics detail leaking into subprocess-teardown correctness.

7. **PTY read/write share one open file and fight over `O_NONBLOCK`.** The write drain flips the descriptor non-blocking, and the read stream can observe `EAGAIN` during that window — which is why the read error handler treats `EAGAIN`/`EWOULDBLOCK` as benign and there's a `masterReadRestartTimer`. Both `close` and `error` paths null out `this.masterReadStream` only if identity matches, and a `restartAfterClose` flag decides whether close means "restart" or "done." Genuinely intricate, and entirely outside the reactive graph.

8. **Inline write drain to save ~1 ms of keystroke latency** (`OpenPty.ts:228-235`) — `setTimeout(…, 0)` is clamped to a whole millisecond, so deferring the common case "spent about a millisecond of keystroke latency to serve the saturated case."

9. **Errors that must not surface synchronously are rethrown on a later turn** — `setTimeout(() => { throw error }, 0)` appears twice in OpenPty (`:181`, `:244`), so a genuine PTY errno becomes an uncaught exception rather than propagating out of a keystroke handler that has no way to handle it.

10. **Negative caching is explicit** — `GitBlameCache` stores an *empty* map for an unblamable file at that mtime, "so the negative result never re-spawns per frame."

11. **`GitWorkspace.renderVersion` returns `Date.now()`** after ~20 `void x.value` subscriptions — a deliberately always-different value so the pane's render-revision port never dedupes.

12. **Doc/code drift in `reconcileLogTip`** (detailed in §4) — `git.invariants.md:359` asserts an observation gate that no longer exists in `src/`.

13. **`GitWatcher` unrefs every timer and the HEAD watcher** (`.unref?.()` at `:98`, `:246`, `:376`) — a pending debounce or the 5 s floor never keeps the process alive at shutdown.

14. Only **9 `$watch`/`$watchEffect` sites in the entire non-test codebase.** Reactivity here is overwhelmingly *pull* (tracked getters read by one coarse frame effect, `app.invariants.md:88` *Rendering is one coarse frame effect*, status **established**), with push reserved for the handful of places where a state change must drive an *action* rather than a repaint: theme apply, glyph mode, panel cycling, tab orientation, dock activation, focus projection (`flush: 'sync'`, with a comment explaining a sidebar-click focus round-trip that a queued watcher coalesced to "no change"), word-wrap re-anchor, and LSP document sync.

